-*- Text -*-

This package is built with crypto support.  The interface to the
crypto code is implemented using dynamically-loaded modules.  This
allows scheme to be run even if the crypto libraries aren't available;
in that case the crypto support is unavailable, but everything else
should work.

In order to build the package, it's preferable to have the following
crypto libraries installed.  It's possible to build the package
without these libraries, or with a subset of them, but in that case
there won't be any crypto support in the resulting package.

	libssl-dev
	libmhash-dev
	libmcrypt-dev

Architectures and bootstrapping
-------------------------------

MIT/GNU Scheme's compiler is written in Scheme, so building the package
requires a working MIT/GNU Scheme on the build machine -- hence the
self-referential build dependency.  Upstream ships native-code back ends
for x86-64 and AArch64 (as well as i386, a portable C back end and the
SVM virtual machine); the packaging enables the x86-64 back end on amd64
and the AArch64 one on arm64.

Upstream's configure only auto-detects the x86 family, and it names its
back ends in its own way -- "x86-64", "aarch64le" -- rather than using
Debian or GNU architecture names.  debian/rules therefore keeps a single
mapping from the Debian architecture to upstream's name, in the BIN_ARCH
variable, and uses it both for --enable-native-code and for the file
names that follow from it: the interpreter is installed as
/usr/bin/mit-scheme-$BIN_ARCH-$VERSION, with an unversioned
/usr/bin/mit-scheme-$BIN_ARCH symlink beside it, and that is what the
mit-scheme and scheme alternatives point at.  The postinst and prerm
repeat the mapping so that they can work it out from
$DPKG_MAINTSCRIPT_ARCH at run time.

Adding an architecture therefore needs an entry in the BIN_ARCH mapping
in debian/rules and matching entries in debian/mit-scheme.postinst and
debian/mit-scheme.prerm, plus the architecture in debian/control.

The awkward part is the bootstrap.  Because the build needs an existing
MIT/GNU Scheme, an architecture cannot build itself until a binary for
it already exists in the archive, and a buildd has nothing to start
from.  The compiler can, however, generate code for an architecture
other than the one it is running on, which is how upstream ports Scheme
to new machines: a cross build, with the compiler running on a machine
that already has Scheme and emitting code for the new target.  The
build dependency is marked mit-scheme:native to say that the dependency
is on the build machine's Scheme, not the host architecture's.

So the first binary for a new architecture has to be produced by hand
and bootstrapped into the archive; after that, ordinary buildd builds
take over.
